      "writtenOn": "2024-02-13T11:12:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t believe that\u0027s true; DTC will generate the `/fragment@X/__overlay__` nodes when compiling an overlay (`/plugin/`).\n\nTo illustrate my point, can\u0027t your example below be written as\n```dts\n/dts-v1/;\n/plugin/\n\n/ {\n    host {\n        // ...\n    };\n};\n\n\u0026{/} { // This only generates one fragment@X; why have 3?\n    rng: rng {\n        compatible \u003d \"android,rng\";\n        android,rng,ignore-gctrl-reset;\n    };\n\n    light: light {\n        compatible \u003d \"android,light\";\n        version \u003d \u003c0x1 0x2\u003e;\n    };\n\n    bus0 {\n        led: led {\n            compatible \u003d \"android,led\";\n            prop \u003d \u003c0x555\u003e;\n        };\n    };\n};\n```\n?\n\nIMO, it would make sense to limit this documentation to DTS examples, as readers are much more likely to be familiar with those than with the nitty-gritty details of DTBOs. Or do you have an example of something we do that actually requires manually generating DTBO internals in DTS?\n\nFor example, below you introduce a requirement in terms of being \"defined under the `/__symbols__`\", which would be more easily understood if expressed as \"having a label\" (with `-@`). Or the whole \"overlayable node\" definition feels pointlessly low-level.\n\nAlso, having small DTS snippets illustrating each requirement would make it easier to follow, IMO.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 27,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 79
      },
      "revId": "d10fcfc4a8dca441c3f10b15ef3258fe62754d27",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
